---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Lesson 8: Sensor-Controlled Outputs with Arduino">
    <main class="min-h-screen bg-gradient-to-br from-blue-50 to-indigo-100 py-12">
        <div class="container mx-auto px-4 max-w-4xl">
            <div class="text-center mb-8">
                <div class="flex items-center justify-center mb-4">
                    <div class="bg-blue-500 text-white rounded-full w-16 h-16 flex items-center justify-center font-bold text-2xl mr-4">8</div>
                    <div class="text-left">
                        <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-800">Lesson 8: Sensor-Controlled Outputs with Arduino</h1>
                        <p class="text-gray-600">Use sensor readings to control LEDs, buzzers, and other outputs</p>
                        <p class="text-sm text-blue-600 font-medium">32-Lesson Course • Semester 1</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-blue-500 rounded-r-lg p-4 mb-8">
                <p class="text-blue-900 font-medium">Now that you can read sensor values with <code>analogRead()</code>, you'll learn to use those values to make decisions and control outputs like LEDs. This combines everything you've learned: conditionals, loops, functions, and sensors.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-lg p-6 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-4">Learning Objectives</h2>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-2">By the end of this Lesson, you will:</h3>
                        <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-700">
                            <li class="flex items-start"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-blue-500 rounded-full mt-2 mr-3 flex-shrink-0"></span>Use sensor values in conditional statements</li>
                            <li class="flex items-start"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-blue-500 rounded-full mt-2 mr-3 flex-shrink-0"></span>Control LEDs based on sensor thresholds</li>
                            <li class="flex items-start"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-blue-500 rounded-full mt-2 mr-3 flex-shrink-0"></span>Write functions that respond to sensor data</li>
                            <li class="flex items-start"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-blue-500 rounded-full mt-2 mr-3 flex-shrink-0"></span>Create a complete sensor-controlled project</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-green-600 mb-2">Skills Developed:</h3>
                        <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-700">
                            <li class="flex items-start"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-green-500 rounded-full mt-2 mr-3 flex-shrink-0"></span>Combining sensors with output devices</li>
                            <li class="flex items-start"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-green-500 rounded-full mt-2 mr-3 flex-shrink-0"></span>Setting thresholds for sensor decisions</li>
                            <li class="flex items-start"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-green-500 rounded-full mt-2 mr-3 flex-shrink-0"></span>Building responsive hardware systems</li>
                            <li class="flex items-start"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-green-500 rounded-full mt-2 mr-3 flex-shrink-0"></span>Debugging sensor-controlled circuits</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-lg p-6 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-6">Section 1: From Input to Output</h2>
                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">In Lesson 7, you learned to read sensor values. Now you'll use those values to control outputs. The pattern is simple: <strong>read → decide → act</strong>.</p>
                <div class="bg-blue-50 p-4 rounded-lg mb-4">
                    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-blue-800 mb-2">The control loop pattern</h3>
                    <ol class="space-y-2 text-blue-900 list-decimal list-inside">
                        <li>Read the sensor with <code>analogRead()</code></li>
                        <li>Compare the value to a threshold (conditionals)</li>
                        <li>Control the output accordingly</li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
                <p class="text-gray-700">This pattern works for any sensor and any output. For example: read a light sensor, check if it's dark, turn on an LED.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-lg p-6 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-6">Section 2: LED Controlled by Potentiometer</h2>
                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Let's start with a simple example: turn on an LED when the potentiometer reads above a certain value.</p>
                <div class="bg-white border border-blue-100 rounded-lg p-5 mb-4">
                    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-blue-800 mb-3">Hardware setup: potentiometer and LED</h3>
                    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
                        <div class="bg-blue-50 p-4 rounded-lg text-blue-900">
                            <h4 class="font-semibold mb-2">Potentiometer (from Lesson 7)</h4>
                            <ul class="text-sm space-y-1">
                                <li>Left pin → <code>5V</code></li>
                                <li>Middle pin → <code>A0</code></li>
                                <li>Right pin → <code>GND</code></li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                        <div class="bg-yellow-50 p-4 rounded-lg text-yellow-900">
                            <h4 class="font-semibold mb-2">LED (with resistor)</h4>
                            <ul class="text-sm space-y-1">
                                <li>Long leg → Pin <code>13</code> (through 220Ω resistor)</li>
                                <li>Short leg → <code>GND</code></li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="relative">
                    <button onclick="copyCode(this)" class="absolute top-2 right-2 bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-700 text-white text-xs px-2 py-1 rounded transition-colors duration-200">
                        📋 Copy
                    </button>
                    <pre style="background: #1a202c; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 6px; overflow-x: auto; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace; margin: 1rem 0;"><code>{`const int sensorPin = A0;
const int ledPin = 13;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
  pinMode(ledPin, OUTPUT);
}

void loop() {
  int sensorValue = analogRead(sensorPin);
  Serial.print("Sensor: ");
  Serial.println(sensorValue);

  if (sensorValue > 500) {
    digitalWrite(ledPin, HIGH);  // LED on
  } else {
    digitalWrite(ledPin, LOW);   // LED off
  }

  delay(200);
}`}</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="bg-green-50 p-4 rounded-lg mt-4">
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-green-800 mb-2">What happens?</h3>
                    <ul class="space-y-2 text-green-700">
                        <li>When <code>sensorValue > 500</code>, the LED turns on</li>
                        <li>When <code>sensorValue &lt;= 500</code>, the LED turns off</li>
                        <li>The threshold (500) is roughly in the middle of the 0-1023 range</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-lg p-6 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-6">Section 3: Choosing a Threshold</h2>
                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">The threshold value (like 500) is important. It determines when your output changes. Here's how to think about it:</p>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg">
                        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-2">Finding a good threshold</h3>
                        <ol class="space-y-2 text-gray-700 text-sm list-decimal list-inside">
                            <li>Run the sensor reading program from Lesson 7</li>
                            <li>Watch the Serial Monitor or Plotter</li>
                            <li>Note the values when the condition is true/false</li>
                            <li>Pick a value in between</li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg">
                        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-2">Example thresholds</h3>
                        <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-700 text-sm">
                            <li>Potentiometer: <code>512</code> (middle)</li>
                            <li>Light sensor: <code>300</code> (dim/dark)</li>
                            <li>Temperature: depends on your sensor</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-lg p-6 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-6">Section 4: Using Functions for Sensor Control</h2>
                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">You can organize your code with functions (from Lesson 6). This makes it easier to read and maintain.</p>
                <div class="relative">
                    <button onclick="copyCode(this)" class="absolute top-2 right-2 bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-700 text-white text-xs px-2 py-1 rounded transition-colors duration-200">
                        📋 Copy
                    </button>
                    <pre style="background: #1a202c; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 6px; overflow-x: auto; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace; margin: 1rem 0;"><code>{`const int sensorPin = A0;
const int ledPin = 13;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
  pinMode(ledPin, OUTPUT);
}

void loop() {
  checkSensorAndControlLED();
  delay(200);
}

void checkSensorAndControlLED() {
  int sensorValue = analogRead(sensorPin);
  Serial.print("Sensor: ");
  Serial.println(sensorValue);

  if (sensorValue > 500) {
    turnOnLED();
  } else {
    turnOffLED();
  }
}

void turnOnLED() {
  digitalWrite(ledPin, HIGH);
  Serial.println("LED is ON");
}

void turnOffLED() {
  digitalWrite(ledPin, LOW);
  Serial.println("LED is OFF");
}`}</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="bg-blue-50 p-4 rounded-lg mt-4 text-blue-900">
                    Using functions makes the code more organized. Each function has a clear job: read the sensor, turn the LED on, or turn the LED off.
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-lg p-6 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-6">Section 5: Multiple Thresholds</h2>
                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">You can use multiple thresholds to create different response levels. For example, show "Low", "Medium", or "High" based on the sensor value.</p>
                <div class="relative">
                    <button onclick="copyCode(this)" class="absolute top-2 right-2 bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-700 text-white text-xs px-2 py-1 rounded transition-colors duration-200">
                        📋 Copy
                    </button>
                    <pre style="background: #1a202c; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 6px; overflow-x: auto; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace; margin: 1rem 0;"><code>{`const int sensorPin = A0;
const int ledPin = 13;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
  pinMode(ledPin, OUTPUT);
}

void loop() {
  int sensorValue = analogRead(sensorPin);
  Serial.print("Sensor: ");
  Serial.print(sensorValue);
  Serial.print(" - Level: ");
  printSensorLevel(sensorValue);
  delay(300);
}

void printSensorLevel(int value) {
  if (value < 341) {
    Serial.println("Low");
    digitalWrite(ledPin, LOW);
  } else if (value < 682) {
    Serial.println("Medium");
    digitalWrite(ledPin, HIGH);
  } else {
    Serial.println("High");
    digitalWrite(ledPin, HIGH);
  }
}`}</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="bg-yellow-50 p-4 rounded-lg mt-4 text-yellow-900">
                    <h3 class="font-semibold mb-2">How it works</h3>
                    <ul class="space-y-2">
                        <li><code>0-340</code>: Low → LED off</li>
                        <li><code>341-681</code>: Medium → LED on</li>
                        <li><code>682-1023</code>: High → LED on</li>
                        <li>You can add more LEDs for visual feedback</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-lg p-6 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-6">Section 6: Looping with Sensors</h2>
                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">You can combine sensor readings with loops (from Lesson 5). For example, take 5 readings and average them for a more stable value.</p>
                <div class="relative">
                    <button onclick="copyCode(this)" class="absolute top-2 right-2 bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-700 text-white text-xs px-2 py-1 rounded transition-colors duration-200">
                        📋 Copy
                    </button>
                    <pre style="background: #1a202c; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 6px; overflow-x: auto; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace; margin: 1rem 0;"><code>{`const int sensorPin = A0;
const int ledPin = 13;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
  pinMode(ledPin, OUTPUT);
}

void loop() {
  int averageValue = getAverageReading();
  Serial.print("Average: ");
  Serial.println(averageValue);
  controlLED(averageValue);
  delay(500);
}

int getAverageReading() {
  int sum = 0;
  for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    sum += analogRead(sensorPin);
    delay(50);
  }
  return sum / 5;
}

void controlLED(int value) {
  if (value > 500) {
    digitalWrite(ledPin, HIGH);
    Serial.println("LED ON");
  } else {
    digitalWrite(ledPin, LOW);
    Serial.println("LED OFF");
  }
}`}</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="bg-green-50 p-4 rounded-lg mt-4 text-green-900">
                    <h3 class="font-semibold mb-2">Why average readings?</h3>
                    <p>Sensor readings can be noisy. Taking multiple readings and averaging them gives a smoother, more stable value. This is a common technique in real-world projects.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-lg p-6 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-6">Section 7: Common Mistakes</h2>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
                    <div class="bg-red-50 p-4 rounded-lg">
                        <h3 class="font-semibold text-red-800 mb-2">Mistake 1</h3>
                        <p class="text-red-700 text-sm">Forgetting <code>pinMode(ledPin, OUTPUT)</code> in <code>setup()</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 p-4 rounded-lg">
                        <h3 class="font-semibold text-red-800 mb-2">Mistake 2</h3>
                        <p class="text-red-700 text-sm">Using the wrong pin number (e.g., <code>A0</code> for LED output instead of a digital pin).</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 p-4 rounded-lg">
                        <h3 class="font-semibold text-red-800 mb-2">Mistake 3</h3>
                        <p class="text-red-700 text-sm">Not using a resistor with the LED, which can damage the LED.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 p-4 rounded-lg">
                        <h3 class="font-semibold text-red-800 mb-2">Mistake 4</h3>
                        <p class="text-red-700 text-sm">Choosing a threshold that's too close to the edge of the range.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-lg p-6 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-6">Hands-On Activity: Light-Activated LED</h2>
                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Create a system that turns on an LED when it's dark. You can use a potentiometer to simulate a light sensor, or if you have a photoresistor, use that instead.</p>
                <div class="bg-yellow-50 p-4 rounded-lg mb-4 text-yellow-900">
                    <h3 class="font-semibold mb-2">Steps:</h3>
                    <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-2">
                        <li>Wire a potentiometer to <code>A0</code> (or use a light sensor)</li>
                        <li>Wire an LED with resistor to pin <code>13</code></li>
                        <li>Write a program that turns the LED on when the sensor reads below a threshold (dark)</li>
                        <li>Test by covering/uncovering the sensor or turning the potentiometer</li>
                        <li>Use functions to organize your code</li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
                <p class="text-gray-700"><strong>Extension:</strong> Add a second LED and create three brightness levels: dim, medium, bright.</p>
                <div class="bg-purple-50 p-4 rounded-lg mt-4 text-purple-900">
                    <h3 class="font-semibold mb-2">Sneak Peek: Variable Brightness</h3>
                    <p>Arduino has special pins (marked with <code>~</code>: 3, 5, 6, 9, 10, 11) that can dim an LED smoothly instead of just on/off. Using <code>analogWrite()</code>, a sensor can control brightness like a dimmer switch. We'll cover this in a future lesson!</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-lg p-6 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-4">Assessment and Homework</h2>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 mb-6">
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-3">Lesson 8 quiz topics</h3>
                        <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-700">
                            <li>Using sensor values in conditionals</li>
                            <li>Choosing thresholds for sensor decisions</li>
                            <li>Controlling LEDs based on sensor input</li>
                            <li>Writing functions for sensor control</li>
                            <li>Using loops with sensor readings</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-3">Homework ideas</h3>
                        <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-700">
                            <li>Create a sensor-controlled LED system</li>
                            <li>Experiment with different threshold values</li>
                            <li>Write a function that returns a sensor "level" (low/medium/high)</li>
                            <li>Try averaging multiple sensor readings</li>
                            <li>Be ready to build a complete project next</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="bg-green-50 p-4 rounded-lg text-green-900">
                    You now have all the pieces: reading sensors, making decisions, and controlling outputs. This is the foundation for building real projects!
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="flex justify-between items-center mt-8">
                <div>
